## Input

```mlk
// A pipeline: a value, the call it goes into, and a place among the arguments of that call
// for the value. The value is bound before the call, so the left side runs first.

fun passed(): Int =
    input |> f(_)

fun placed(): Int =
    input |> f(1, _)

fun twice(): Int =
    input |> f(_, _)

// A step written with the arrow is a call as any other is, and the receiver is passed first:
// this one passes `map`, `"k"` and the value.
fun inserted(): Int =
    input |> map->insert("k", _)

// The pipeline is the loosest operator: the sum is what the first step takes. What is written
// after a step is written after the value the pipeline is, so `prepare` is called on what
// `make-data` produced, and the `|>` after it is a step of that value.
fun chained(): Int =
    a + b
    |> make-data(_)
    ->prepare()
    |> map->insert("k", _)

// A step is one call: a call on the result of another call goes inside the arguments.
fun nested(): Int =
    input |> g(f(1), _)

// A step written inside the arguments of another one binds its own value, and a place of the
// outer step is the outer value.
fun inner(): Int =
    input |> f(other |> g(_), _)

// A field read is what may follow a pipeline as well: the name is read on the value the step
// produced.
fun field(): Int =
    input |> f(_).field

// A pipeline is an expression like any other: it is what a call passes where a value belongs.
fun argument(): Int =
    h(input |> f(_))

```

